{"qid":"Q2247225","names":["Tarumanagara"],"title":"Tarumanagara","endonym":null,"capsule":"Tarumanagara was one of the earliest Indianized Hindu kingdoms of western Java, centered on the Citarum and Ciliwung river basins in the region of modern Jakarta and Bogor, attested from the fourth century CE. Its most celebrated ruler, King Purnawarman (c. 395–434), left seven Sanskrit stone inscriptions—the oldest known written records of Java—associating himself with Vishnu, recording canal construction, and donating cattle to Brahmin priests. The kingdom maintained trade links with southern Indian states and the Gupta world. It collapsed around 669 CE following a Srivijayan military campaign, with its territory dissolving into the successor kingdoms of Sunda and Galuh.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Tarumanagara's core territory around the Citarum basin is inferred from the findspots of Purnawarman's inscriptions; the kingdom's full extent is uncertain and scholarly estimates vary considerably.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Tarumanagara","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarumanagara"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Purnawarman","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Purnawarman"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Kingdom of Sunda","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Sunda"}],"britannica":"place/Tarumanegara","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Tarumanagara","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Jayasingawarman","nativeName":"Jayasingawarman","roman":"Jayasingawarman","start":367,"end":382,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Dharmayawarman","nativeName":"Dharmayawarman","roman":"Dha